<sn><md>### Importance

+++ view
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/General%20Science/B.Z.Food%20and%20Nutrition/assets/importance_of_water.png)
+++

* **Body Composition:** Water constitutes `60–75%` of total body weight.
* **Tissue Formation:** Crucial for the formation of `blood, muscle, nerves, teeth, and bones`.
* **Cellular &amp; Physiological Function:** Essential for forming `body cells` and enabling `physiological functions`.
* **Solvent &amp; Digestion:** Acts as a `body solvent`, aiding in the `digestion and absorption` of food.
* **Waste Elimination:** Helps `excrete toxic metabolic byproducts` (such as urea and ammonia) through urine and sweat.
* **Thermoregulation:** Keeps the ++body cool++ via `sweating and evaporation`.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Water Deficiency &amp; Remedies

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-md-6
**Causes:** 
- Inadequate fluid intake, 
- hot weather, strenuous labor
- uncontrolled diabetes
- excessive vomiting or diarrhea.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-md-6
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/General%20Science/B.Z.Food%20and%20Nutrition/assets/dehydration.png)
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-md-6
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/General%20Science/B.Z.Food%20and%20Nutrition/assets/consequences_of_dehydration.png)
:::

:::.col-12 .col-md-6
**Symptoms &amp; Consequences:**
* Severe `thirst`, `shrunken skin`, and `inhibited blood circulation`.
* Weakness in `nerves and muscles`.
* Loss of ++acid-alkali balance++ leading to `acidosis`.
* A ++10% loss of body water++ can cause `fainting or death`.
:::
:::

</md></sn><sn><md>#### Treatment and Oral Rehydration

Rapid recovery from water and electrolyte loss requires salt water or oral saline solution:

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/General%20Science/B.Z.Food%20and%20Nutrition/assets/saline_preparation.png)

* **Homemade Oral Saline Recipe:** If commercial oral saline is unavailable, mix a pinch of salt, a handful of molasses or sugar, and a glass of boiled, room-temperature water.</md></sn>